Snow Queen","targetName":"The Snow Queen","context":"Andersen drew on traditional Nordic myths and legends in crafting the magical creatures that inhabit The Snow Queen's world."},{"sourceSlug":"Feminist scholarship","sourceName":"Feminist scholarship","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The Snow Queen","targetName":"The Snow Queen","context":"Scholars have interpreted The Snow Queen as a commentary on the social and cultural norms of Andersen's time, particularly with regards to gender roles and identity."},{"sourceSlug":"LGBTQ+ rights discourse","sourceName":"LGBTQ+ rights discourse","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The Snow Queen","targetName":"The Snow Queen","context":"Andersen's work has also been interpreted as a commentary on LGBTQ+ identity and experience."},{"sourceSlug":"Climate change activism","sourceName":"Climate change activism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The Snow Queen","targetName":"The Snow Queen","context":"The tale has been used as a metaphor for climate change activism, highlighting the importance of protecting the natural world."}],"places":[{"name":"Copenhagen","role":"Birthplace"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Snow Queen","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-snow-queen"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q11880","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Snow_Queen"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"The Snow Queen contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Snow Queen (Danish: Snedronningen) is a fairy tale by Hans Christian Andersen (1805–1875), first published on 21 December 1844 in the fifth and final volume of Eventyr fortalte for Børn ('Fairy Tales Told for Children'), alongside The Fir Tree. The Snow Queen is Andersen's longest fairy tale and is widely regarded as one of his greatest — a seven-part narrative of love, adventure, and the rescue of a boy named Kai from the Snow Queen's palace by his friend (and in some readings, his true love) Gerda. Kai's heart has been pierced by a shard of a demon's magic mirror that makes him see only the bad and ugly in everything; Gerda's journey through seasons and kingdoms to find him — meeting a sorceress, robbers, a reindeer, and various magical helpers — ends in the Snow Queen's icy palace, where Gerda's tears and love dissolve the mirror shards from Kai's heart and eye.

The Snow Queen is an original Andersen tale — unlike his other fairy tales based on oral sources (Thumbelina, The Little Mermaid, The Wild Swans), The Snow Queen is largely Andersen's own invention, drawing loosely on Danish winter mythology but primarily on his own imagination and life experience. Scholars have identified autobiographical elements — the contrast between cold rationality (Kai's mirror-shard view) and warm emotion (Gerda's faithful love) has been read as reflecting Andersen's own creative conflict; and Kai and Gerda's friendship has been read as reflecting Andersen's relationships with his childhood friend Riborg Voigt and his complex emotional life.

The Snow Queen achieved global cultural reach through Disney's Frozen (2013) — a loose adaptation that made the Snow Queen story the highest-grossing animated film ever made at the time of its release, with the song 'Let It Go' becoming a global cultural phenomenon and the film's themes of sisterhood and female empowerment representing a significant departure from the Andersen original. The commercial and cultural impact of Frozen has made the Snow Queen one of the three or four most recognised Andersen tales worldwide.","subjects":["Danish Literature","19th Century","Fairy Tale","Hans Christian Andersen","Fantasy Literature","Children's Literature","Disney Animation","Folk Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Snow Queen has had a lasting impact on children's literature and continues to be widely read and adapted today.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
